<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<section xml:id="sec-6-2-oop-intro">
  <title>What is Object-Oriented Programming?</title>

  <p>
    Programming languages fall into different approaches<mdash/>or <em>paradigms</em><mdash/>for 
    solving computational problems, including procedural (such as C and Pascal), 
    functional (Haskell, Clojure), object-oriented (Java, C#), and multi-paradigm 
    (C++, Python). Each paradigm offers its own methodology for organizing software.
  </p>

  <p>
    In Chapter 5, we solved problems using a procedural (functional decomposition) approach. 
    In procedural programming, data and behavior are kept separate: data is stored in local 
    variables inside <c>main</c>, and we pass those values back and forth as arguments 
    to <c>static</c> helper methods. You will still use procedural decomposition in 
    object-oriented programming, but on a smaller scale<mdash/>when writing the code 
    inside individual methods.
  </p>

  <p>
    On a larger scale, object-oriented programming decomposes a system into objects that 
    collaborate to solve a problem. Rather than separating data from the methods that 
    process it, an object bundles its own data (called its <em>state</em>) together with 
    the operations it can perform (called its <em>behavior</em>).
  </p>

  <p>
    Much of the software you use every day is composed of objects, behind the scenes. 
    For example, when you interact with a graphical application or mobile app, each 
    visual control on the screen<mdash/>such as a button, a text box, or a slider<mdash/>is 
    represented internally as an object. Each object is responsible for maintaining its 
    own state and carrying out its own behavior. A button object knows what text or icon 
    it displays and how to respond when clicked; a text box object knows what characters 
    have been typed into it and can provide that text to other parts of the program on request.
  </p>

  <p>
    In fact, you have been programming with objects since Chapter 2. Every <c>String</c>, 
    <c>Scanner</c>, <c>File</c>, and <c>PrintWriter</c> you have used in your programs is 
    an object. When you wrote <c>cityFile.exists()</c>, you asked a <c>File</c> object to 
    check its own internal path and report whether that file existed on disk.
  </p>

  <p>
    Decomposing a system into interacting objects provides two immediate software 
    engineering benefits:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Encapsulation:</strong> Bundling data (state) and the operations on that 
        data (behavior) together into a single unit<mdash/>an object. Encapsulation makes 
        development and testing easier because an object is a self-contained component 
        that can be built and unit-tested independently. It also simplifies maintenance, 
        making it much easier to locate and update logic in a specific component than to 
        hunt through a sprawling, monolithic program.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <p>
        <strong>Information hiding:</strong> Concealing an object's internal details 
        from the rest of the software system. In practice, this means hiding the object's 
        internal data variables and allowing other parts of the program to interact with 
        the object strictly through well-defined methods. Just as you did not need to 
        know how a <c>Scanner</c> buffers bytes in memory to call <c>nextInt()</c>, hiding 
        internal state protects data from accidental corruption. While some textbooks treat 
        information hiding as a required part of encapsulation, separating the two helps 
        explain why some languages (like Python) bundle data and methods together without 
        enforcing strict private access, whereas Java uses both.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Object-oriented design offers additional advantages such as extensibility through 
    inheritance mechanisms. We will explore inheritance, polymorphism, and dynamic 
    binding in future chapters, but you will get a taste of the advantages in 
    <xref ref="sec-6-8-tostring-equals"/>. To begin building our own objects, we first need 
    to learn how to write a class: the blueprint that defines what state and behavior an 
    object will possess.
  </p>
</section>